{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Functional images, complex images, and the CMS-default trap</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      An image inside a link or button, an icon-only "delete" button, say, needs alt text describing the action it performs, not what the icon looks like: <code>alt="Delete"</code>, not <code>alt="trash can icon"</code>. The user needs to know what clicking it does, not what it depicts.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A chart or infographic usually holds more information than a short <code>alt</code> string can comfortably carry. The fix isn't cramming a paragraph into <code>alt</code> — it's keeping <code>alt</code> short (what the image shows, in brief) and putting the full data somewhere a screen reader already reaches: an adjacent data table, a caption, or text linked via <code>aria-describedby</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Watch for this pattern specifically: a CMS or component library that defaults every image without manually-entered text to <code>alt=""</code>. That default is correct for images that are genuinely decorative and silently wrong for every other image an editor forgets to fill in, and because both outcomes render identically on screen, this class of bug survives visual QA indefinitely. Audit for it by grepping rendered HTML for <code>alt=""</code> on images inside content areas, not template chrome, rather than by looking at the page.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

