{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">alt="" and a missing alt attribute are not the same thing — only one of them means "skip this"; the other makes some screen readers announce the filename instead.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Ask one question per image: does it convey information the surrounding text doesn't already say? Yes — write real alt text. No — alt="". Never leave the attribute off entirely.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">The failure mode is an implicit default doing the wrong thing silently and identically on screen either way — audit for missing alt attributes and blanket CMS-default alt="" separately, since they hide opposite problems and neither shows up in a visual review.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
